  "query_preview": "(This evening through Wednesday)...\nIssued at 149 PM EST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nThis afternoon through Saturday...\n\nExpect quiet weather conditions to persist as strong upper ridging \nand surface high pressure remain in control across the region \nthrough the end of the week. Model guidance depicts a low pressure \nsystem lifting northeastward across the High Plains and northern \nGreat Lakes into Thursday, but the system will remain well to the \nnorth which limits any chance for precipitation. Persistent S/SW \nsurface flow on the western periphery of the aforementioned surface \nhigh is likely going to gradually warm temperatures over the next \nfew days. While highs are expected to be well above normal in the \n60s, efficient radiational cooling conditions should support \ncrisp lows around the mid 30s to low 40s.\n\nSaturday night through next week...\n\nAn approaching upper trough and associated low pressure system will \nbring a pattern shift late this weekend from the warmer and drier \nconditions experienced most of the work week. Ensemble guidance \ndepicts surface cyclogenesis near portions of the Upper Midwest or \nGreat Lakes Region late Saturday night into Sunday, remaining well \nnorth of central Indiana. An associated cold front is expected to \npush through central Indiana on Sunday supporting the potential for \nprecipitation. However, limited forcing due to the system being \ndisplaced to the north should keep QPF amounts light. A relatively \nnarrow corridor of sufficient moisture return along or just ahead of \nthe cold front may also limit precipitation coverage. \n\nPrecipitation chances quickly diminish late Sunday behind the \ndeparting front with cooler and drier air filtering in. Look for \nsurface high pressure to build back in promoting quiet weather \nconditions. Meanwhile, CAA will cool temperatures significantly. \nHighs near the lower 60s on Sunday are expected to cool well into \nthe 40s early next week. Lows are likely going to range from the mid \n20s to low 30s Monday night through Wednesday night. \n\nEnsemble guidance begins to diverge considerably from next Wednesday \nonward which leads to higher uncertainty in the forecast. That being \nsaid, long range guidance generally depicts an amplifying deep \ntrough across the central CONUS late next week. Surface cyclogenesis \nappears increasingly likely upstream of the trough, but confidence \nin exactly where the surface low develops remains low due to \ndiverging model solutions. This system will be worth monitoring into \nthe weekend after Thanksgiving as some guidance suggest strong \nsurface cyclogenesis could occur. Specific details will remain \nhighly uncertain until models become better aligned.",
